      content:
          'A 52-year-old right-hand-dominant female is referred to the lymphedema clinic with progressive right upper extremity swelling 2 years after right modified radical mastectomy with axillary lymph node dissection (ALND, 18 of 22 nodes removed, 4 positive) and adjuvant radiation to the right chest wall and axilla for Stage IIIA (T2N2M0) invasive ductal carcinoma. She noticed mild hand swelling 6 months ago that she attributed to warm weather. Over the past 3 months, the swelling has progressed to involve the entire right arm and is now constantly present. She has had two episodes of cellulitis in the past year requiring oral antibiotics. She reports heaviness, tightness, and intermittent aching in the arm. She has stopped attending her book club and social gatherings, telling her husband she is embarrassed by the appearance of her arm. She works as a high school art teacher and is having difficulty demonstrating painting techniques.',
