    Section(
      title: '+ Stage this patient\'s lymphedema using the ISL classification. What diagnostic workup should be performed, and what are the key differential diagnoses?',
      content:
          '**International Society of Lymphology (ISL) Staging**:\n- **Stage 0 (Subclinical)**: Lymphatic damage present but no visible swelling. Detectable by bioimpedance or lymphoscintigraphy.\n- **Stage I (Spontaneously reversible)**: Early accumulation of protein-rich fluid. Pitting edema that reduces with elevation. Subsides with overnight rest.\n- **Stage II Early**: Limb elevation alone rarely reduces swelling. Pitting is present.\n- **Stage II Late**: Progressive tissue fibrosis. May or may not pit. Skin changes beginning.\n- **Stage III (Lymphostatic elephantiasis)**: Pitting may be absent. Skin changes — papillomas, hyperkeratosis, massive volume increase.\n\n**This Patient: ISL Stage II Late** — She has a combination of pitting edema (hand/forearm) and non-pitting fibrotic tissue (upper arm), positive Stemmer sign, peau d\'orange skin changes, and the swelling does not resolve with elevation or overnight rest. She is transitioning toward Stage III without intervention.\n\n**Severity by Volume**: >40% volume excess = **severe** lymphedema.\n\n**Required Diagnostic Workup**:\n1. **Duplex ultrasound of right upper extremity**: Rule out **deep vein thrombosis (DVT)** — essential before initiating compression therapy. Upper extremity DVT can occur in cancer patients (especially with central lines, radiation).\n2. **Cross-sectional imaging**: Review recent PET/CT for any evidence of axillary or supraclavicular nodal recurrence that could cause lymphatic obstruction. If >6 months since last scan, consider updated imaging.\n3. **Bioimpedance spectroscopy (L-Dex)**: Measures extracellular fluid volume. L-Dex >10 suggests significant lymphedema. Useful for ongoing monitoring and documenting treatment response.\n4. **Lymphoscintigraphy**: If diagnosis is uncertain or to plan surgical intervention. Shows delayed or absent lymphatic transport in the affected limb.\n5. **Basic labs**: CBC (rule out infection), CMP, albumin (hypoalbuminemia causes generalized edema), TSH (hypothyroidism can cause edema).\n\n**Key Differential Diagnoses**:\n1. **Cancer recurrence** with lymphatic obstruction — most critical to rule out\n2. **Deep vein thrombosis** — can coexist with lymphedema\n3. **Cellulitis** — recurrent in lymphedema; no current signs\n4. **Radiation-induced brachial plexopathy** with autonomic changes — no neurologic signs here\n5. **Medication-induced edema** — review medication list\n6. **Chronic venous insufficiency** — typically lower extremity, but can contribute\n7. **Heart failure or nephrotic syndrome** — bilateral edema expected, not unilateral\n8. **Lipedema** — bilateral, symmetric, spares hands and feet, non-pitting',
