    Section(
      title: '+ The patient had a bioimpedance reading of L-Dex 42 at today\'s visit. Her baseline pre-surgical L-Dex was 2. Explain bioimpedance spectroscopy and its role in lymphedema surveillance.',
      content:
          '**Bioimpedance Spectroscopy (BIS) — L-Dex Technology**:\n\n**Mechanism**: BIS measures the impedance (resistance) of tissue to a small, painless electrical current passed between electrodes placed on the hands and feet. Extracellular fluid (edema) has low impedance, while intracellular fluid has higher impedance. By measuring at multiple frequencies, BIS can **differentiate extracellular fluid from total body water**, providing an objective measure of lymphedema.\n\n**L-Dex Score Interpretation**:\n- Compares the affected limb\'s impedance ratio to the contralateral limb\n- **Normal range**: -10 to +10\n- **L-Dex >10**: Subclinical or clinical lymphedema likely\n- **L-Dex >20**: Established lymphedema\n- **This patient\'s L-Dex of 42**: Severe lymphedema with marked extracellular fluid accumulation, consistent with ISL Stage II late\n\n**Role in Prospective Surveillance Model (PSM)**:\n1. **Pre-surgical baseline**: Measure L-Dex before any breast cancer surgery (this patient\'s baseline was 2 — normal).\n2. **Serial monitoring**: Measure at each follow-up visit (every 3-6 months post-surgery). An increase of >10 from individual baseline triggers early intervention.\n3. **Early detection advantage**: BIS can detect subclinical lymphedema (Stage 0/I) **before clinically visible swelling**, allowing early compression intervention that can prevent progression.\n4. **Treatment response tracking**: Serial L-Dex measurements objectively document response to CDT, compliance with compression, and detect early recurrence.\n\n**Limitations**:\n- Requires contralateral limb for comparison (bilateral lymphedema cannot be assessed)\n- Affected by body composition changes, hydration status, and implanted devices (pacemakers are a contraindication)\n- Does not distinguish lymphedema from other causes of edema\n\n**Evidence**: The PREVENT trial demonstrated that prospective BIS surveillance with early compression intervention reduced the rate of clinical lymphedema progression by approximately 50% compared to standard follow-up. This has shifted the paradigm toward **prospective surveillance rather than reactive treatment**.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT',
