    Section(
      title: '+ Outline the phases of Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT) and discuss advanced interventions for refractory lymphedema.',
      content:
          '**Complete Decongestive Therapy (CDT) — Standard of Care for Moderate-Severe Lymphedema**:\n\n**Phase I — Intensive Reduction Phase (2-6 weeks)**:\n1. **Manual Lymphatic Drainage (MLD)**: Specialized light-pressure massage technique that redirects lymph fluid from congested areas to functional lymph node basins. Performed by a **certified lymphedema therapist (CLT)** 4-5 days/week. Starts proximally (clearing trunk and unaffected quadrants first), then progresses distally.\n2. **Short-stretch compression bandaging**: Multi-layer bandaging applied after each MLD session. Uses **short-stretch bandages** (low resting pressure, high working pressure) — NOT elastic ACE wraps. Includes foam padding for even pressure distribution. Worn 23 hours/day, rewrapped daily.\n3. **Remedial exercises**: Performed while bandaged to enhance lymphatic pumping. Includes diaphragmatic breathing, shoulder pumps, fist clenching, arm elevation exercises. Low-intensity, repetitive movements.\n4. **Meticulous skin care**: Daily moisturizing with low-pH lotion. Inspection for cuts, fungal infection, or signs of cellulitis. Nail care to prevent ingrown nails. Goal: prevent cellulitis, which worsens lymphedema with each episode.\n\n**Expected Phase I Outcome**: 40-60% reduction in excess limb volume over 2-6 weeks.\n\n**Phase II — Maintenance Phase (lifelong)**:\n1. **Custom compression garment**: Flat-knit, custom-measured compression sleeve + gauntlet/glove. **30-40 mmHg** (Class II) or **40-50 mmHg** (Class III) depending on severity. Worn during all waking hours. Replaced every 6 months.\n2. **Self-MLD**: Patient trained to perform simplified MLD daily.\n3. **Compression garment wear**: Daytime compression sleeve + nighttime compression bandaging or alternative nighttime garment system (e.g., Solaris or JoViPak).\n4. **Exercise**: Regular aerobic and resistance exercise WITH compression garment. Progressive resistance training is safe and does NOT worsen lymphedema (ACOSOG Z0011, PAL trial).\n5. **Skin care**: Ongoing daily moisturizing and skin inspection.\n6. **Weight management**: Critical — obesity is a modifiable risk factor for lymphedema progression.\n\n**Adjunctive Therapies**:\n- **Intermittent pneumatic compression (IPC)**: Multi-chamber sequential pump used 30-60 minutes daily at home. Supplements CDT but does NOT replace it. Covered by most insurance with prior authorization.\n- **Low-level laser therapy**: May reduce limb volume and tissue fibrosis. 2-3 sessions/week during active treatment.\n- **Kinesiology taping**: Decongestive taping applied between CDT sessions for mild additional fluid movement.\n\n**Surgical Options for Refractory Cases**:\n1. **Lymphovenous anastomosis (LVA)**: Microsurgical procedure connecting lymphatic channels directly to venules. Best results in early-stage lymphedema with functioning lymphatics. Minimally invasive, can be performed under local anesthesia.\n2. **Vascularized lymph node transfer (VLNT)**: Harvest functional lymph nodes (commonly from groin or submental region) and transplant to the affected axilla. Promotes lymphangiogenesis. Best for patients without functional proximal lymphatics.\n3. **Suction-assisted protein lipectomy (SAPL/liposuction)**: For chronic Stage II-III lymphedema where excess tissue is primarily adipose/fibrotic rather than fluid. Requires lifelong strict compression garment use after surgery.\n\n**For This Patient**: Start Phase I CDT immediately (4-5 sessions/week for 4-6 weeks), then transition to Phase II maintenance. Add IPC for home use. Refer to a microsurgeon experienced in lymphatic surgery for evaluation if CDT response is suboptimal after 6 months.',
    ),
