    Section(
      title: '+ Discuss CLT certification requirements, insurance coverage challenges for lymphedema care, and how to address access barriers.',
      content:
          '**Certified Lymphedema Therapist (CLT) Requirements**:\n- Must be a licensed healthcare professional (PT, OT, RN, MD, or massage therapist in some states)\n- Complete a minimum **135-hour certification course** accredited by LANA (Lymphology Association of North America) or equivalent body\n- Curriculum covers: anatomy and pathophysiology of the lymphatic system, CDT techniques (MLD, bandaging, exercise, skin care), assessment and measurement, compression garment selection, and clinical practicum\n- **LANA certification**: Voluntary board certification exam. CLT-LANA designation is the gold standard. Requires recertification every 6 years.\n- **Why this matters**: Non-certified providers may apply incorrect techniques (standard massage is contraindicated, elastic wraps are ineffective). Refer ONLY to CLT-certified therapists.\n\n**Insurance Coverage — Current Landscape**:\n1. **CDT therapy sessions**: Generally covered under outpatient PT/OT benefits. Document diagnosis G97.2 (postprocedural lymphedema) and specific functional limitations. May require prior authorization.\n2. **Compression garments**: This is a **major coverage gap**. Medicare historically does NOT cover compression garments (no benefit category). Many commercial plans also exclude them. However, the **Lymphedema Treatment Act (signed into law 2022, effective 2024)** now requires Medicare to cover compression supplies including garments, bandaging systems, and pneumatic devices.\n3. **Pneumatic compression devices**: Covered by Medicare under DME benefit with documentation of medical necessity (trial of CDT, measurements showing persistent edema). Requires prior authorization and face-to-face evaluation documentation.\n4. **Custom garments cost**: \$200-800 per garment (sleeve + gauntlet/glove), needed bilaterally if both arms affected, replaced every 6 months. Financial burden for uninsured or underinsured patients.\n\n**Addressing Access Barriers for This Patient**:\n1. **Telehealth hybrid model**: Intensive Phase I CDT requires in-person visits. Consider concentrated 4-week daily treatment block (patient stays near clinic or arranges temporary housing). Then transition to telehealth monitoring for Phase II maintenance with periodic in-person reassessments.\n2. **Training a local therapist**: If a local PT/OT is interested, provide mentorship while they pursue CLT certification. The patient\'s community benefits long-term.\n3. **Self-management education**: Invest heavily in Phase II self-MLD training, self-bandaging, and garment donning/doffing education. Ensure patient is independent before discharge from intensive phase.\n4. **Patient advocacy resources**: National Lymphedema Network (NLN), Lymphatic Education & Research Network (LE&RN), and the Lymphedema Advocacy Group offer financial assistance programs, garment banks, and peer support.\n5. **Survivorship clinic integration**: Embed lymphedema screening and management into the cancer survivorship program. Prospective surveillance with BIS at every oncology follow-up to catch recurrence or worsening early.',
