    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'Near the end of the evaluation, the patient becomes tearful and says: \'I can\'t wear anything nice with this arm. I haven\'t worn my wedding ring in months. My husband says he doesn\'t care, but I see him look away. I stopped going to book club because I don\'t want people to see me like this. I feel like cancer took my body and now lymphedema is taking my life.\'',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How would you address this patient\'s body image distress, social isolation, and psychosocial impact of lymphedema?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Validate the emotional weight of lymphedema as a visible, chronic condition**:\n\'What you are describing is something I hear from many of my lymphedema patients, and I want you to know that your feelings are completely valid. Lymphedema is not just swelling — it is a constant, visible reminder of everything you have been through, and it affects how you see yourself and how you move through the world. That is a real and legitimate burden.\'\n\n**2. Acknowledge the specific losses she named**:\n\'Not being able to wear your wedding ring, changing how you dress, pulling away from your book club — each of those is a loss. You are not being vain or dramatic. These are the things that make up your daily life and identity, and lymphedema has intruded on all of them.\'\n\n**3. Offer concrete hope tied to the treatment plan**:\n\'Here is what I want you to know: with proper treatment, we typically see a 40 to 60 percent reduction in arm volume during the intensive phase. That means your arm can get significantly smaller. Many of my patients are able to wear their rings again, wear short sleeves comfortably, and return to activities they had given up. I cannot promise it will go back to exactly how it was, but I can tell you there is a lot of room for improvement from where you are today.\'\n\n**4. Address the relationship concern with sensitivity**:\n\'What you said about your husband looking away — that must be painful. Sometimes our partners do not know how to respond, and their discomfort may come from not wanting to say the wrong thing rather than from how they feel about you. Would you be open to having him come to one of your therapy sessions? It can help partners understand what you are going through and learn how they can support you. Couples often feel closer after that.\'\n\n**5. Create a pathway back to social engagement**:\n\'Regarding your book club — I understand the instinct to pull away, but isolation tends to make everything feel worse. As we start treatment and you see your arm respond, I would like you to set a goal for when you will go back. It does not have to be next week, but having that target gives us something to work toward together. In the meantime, there are also lymphedema support groups where you can connect with women going through the same thing.\'\n\n**6. Address depression formally**:\n\'Your PHQ-9 score today suggests moderate depression, which is not surprising given everything you are carrying. I would like to refer you to a psychologist who specializes in working with cancer survivors. Treatment for depression will also help you engage more fully in your lymphedema therapy and overall recovery.\'\n\n**7. Practical wardrobe solutions**:\n\'There are also some practical things that can help right now. Compression garment companies make fashionable sleeves in various patterns and colors. Some women find that wearing a garment they feel good about actually helps them feel more confident. I can connect you with resources for that.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Validation of body image distress without minimizing, concrete hope tied to evidence-based treatment outcomes, addressing relationship dynamics, graduated social re-engagement planning, formal depression screening and treatment, practical problem-solving for daily dignity.',
    ),
